// L4
mc(4, 'levels', 'Two populations of a medicinal plant growing in different mountain ranges have different amounts of the same active chemical. This is', 'genetic diversity', ['species diversity', 'ecological diversity', 'community diversity'], 'Variation within a species is genetic diversity, like reserpine in <i>Rauwolfia vomitoria</i>.');
mc(4, 'patterns', 'In the species-area relationship, small regions in any group of organisms have slopes of', '0.1 to 0.2', ['0.6 to 1.2', '2 to 3', 'zero'], 'Within a region, Z is 0.1-0.2. Over continents it is steeper (0.6-1.2).');
mc(4, 'patterns', 'Why is Z steeper for entire continents than for smaller areas?', 'Larger areas include different habitats and many species with limited ranges', ['They have fewer species', 'They have no rain', 'Species are lost as area increases'], 'Different habitats and regions add more new species as the area grows.');
mc(4, 'patterns', 'If an area is doubled, with Z around 0.2, the number of species', 'rises by a small proportion only', ['doubles', 'halves', 'does not change'], 'S ∝ A<sup>Z</sup>, and 2<sup>0.2</sup> is about 1.15.', 'A small Z means species increase much more slowly than area.');
mc(4, 'importance', 'Which one of the following statements about Tilman\'s experiments is correct?', 'They showed that the total biomass was more stable and productivity higher in species-rich plots', ['More species caused more variation', 'There was no change', 'Productivity fell in species-rich plots'], 'This supports the idea that diversity helps the stability of communities.');
mc(4, 'importance', 'Why does the textbook say a rich biodiversity can protect against the effects of drought or an invasion?', 'A community with more species is more stable and resists such stress better', ['More species mean more competition only', 'Species diversity has no effect on stress', 'Fewer species give more food'], 'Stable communities resist occasional disturbances such as drought and invasions.');
mc(4, 'importance', 'Bioprospecting is', 'the exploration of molecular, genetic and species-level diversity for products of economic importance', ['the production of crops', 'the mining of ores', 'protection of forests only'], 'Nations with rich biodiversity can derive economic benefits from bioprospecting.');
mc(4, 'loss', 'Which of these were hunted to extinction mainly through over-exploitation?', 'Steller\'s sea cow and passenger pigeon', ['Dodo and Lake Victoria cichlids', 'Quagga and Nile perch', 'Thylacine and Parthenium'], 'Over-exploitation was behind the loss of Steller\'s sea cow and the passenger pigeon.', 'Cichlids went extinct through an introduced predator, not hunting.');
mc(4, 'loss', 'The statement "mammals needing large territories and migratory birds are badly affected" refers to the effect of', 'habitat fragmentation', ['alien invasion', 'coextinction', 'cryopreservation'], 'When habitats are broken into small patches, animals needing large ranges and migratory species lose.');
mc(4, 'loss', 'The "evil quartet" consists of', 'habitat loss and fragmentation, over-exploitation, alien species invasions and co-extinctions', ['pollution, fire, drought and flood', 'hunting, fishing, mining and farming', 'only habitat loss'], 'These are the four major causes of biodiversity loss.');
mc(4, 'loss', 'Which of these is the most important cause of biodiversity loss?', 'Habitat loss and fragmentation', ['Over-exploitation', 'Alien species invasions', 'Co-extinctions'], 'Habitat loss is the main cause of animal and plant extinctions.');
mc(4, 'loss', 'The sixth extinction is expected to be different from the earlier five in that', 'its rate is 100 to 1000 times faster and it is due to human activity', ['it is caused by glaciation', 'it is slower than before', 'it is due to asteroid impact'], 'The current rate is estimated to be 100-1000 times higher than before humans.');
mc(4, 'conservation', 'Biodiversity hotspots are identified on the basis of', 'a high level of species richness and a high degree of endemism', ['a large area', 'high human population', 'high rainfall only'], 'Endemism and richness, along with threat, are used to define a hotspot.');
mc(4, 'conservation', 'The hotspots of the world cover', 'less than 2 per cent of the Earth\'s land area', ['more than 50 per cent', 'about 20 per cent', 'about 10 per cent'], 'Strict protection of the hotspots could reduce extinctions by a very large fraction.');
mc(4, 'conservation', 'Which statement about ex situ conservation is correct?', 'Threatened species are protected outside their natural habitat, as in zoos and seed banks', ['It conserves the whole ecosystem', 'It needs no human care', 'It is done only for plants'], 'Ex situ means off site. Zoos, botanical gardens, seed banks and cryopreservation protect species or genes but not the whole ecosystem.');
mc(4, 'conservation', 'Which is an advantage of in situ conservation over ex situ?', 'It conserves the whole ecosystem and the natural interactions', ['It needs no habitat', 'It stores gametes', 'It makes cloning easy'], 'In situ protects the habitat and the interactions among the species.');
mc(4, 'conservation', 'Gametes of threatened species are preserved by cryopreservation at', '-196 °C in liquid nitrogen', ['0 °C in ice', '37 °C in an incubator', '100 °C in steam'], 'Liquid nitrogen at -196 °C stops all cell activity so gametes can be kept for long.');
asr(4, 'patterns', 'The slope Z is steeper for very large areas.', 'Larger areas include more habitats and many species with limited ranges.', 0, 'The extra habitats add species faster.');
asr(4, 'importance', 'Loss of species from a community can affect ecosystem stability.', 'Species are linked in interactions like rivets in an aircraft.', 0, 'Loss of many species may weaken the whole system.');
asr(4, 'loss', 'Co-extinction can lead to the loss of species that were not directly threatened.', 'A parasite and its host depend on one another.', 0, 'When a host is lost, its specific parasite also goes.');
asr(4, 'conservation', 'Hotspots are given priority for in situ conservation.', 'They have many endemic species and are under threat.', 0, 'High endemism and high threat mean the greatest gain from protection.');
asr(4, 'loss', 'The decline of the cichlid fishes of Lake Victoria was due to over-exploitation by fishermen.', 'A predatory fish, the Nile perch, was introduced into the lake.', 3, 'The cichlids were lost because of the introduced predator (alien species invasion), so A is false. R is a true statement.', 'Look for the main cause: it is alien invasion.');
stm(4, 'patterns', 'Z is between 0.1 and 0.2 for small areas.', 'Z is the slope of the log-log line of species richness against area.', 0, 'Both are correct.');
stm(4, 'loss', 'The sixth mass extinction is due to human activities.', 'Earlier extinctions were due to human activities.', 2, 'The earlier five were natural events. Statement II is false.');
stm(4, 'conservation', 'Sacred groves are a kind of in situ conservation.', 'In these groves, all the trees are cut for religious use.', 2, 'The trees and animals are protected there, so Statement II is false.');
stm(4, 'importance', 'The ethical argument holds that species have a right to live.', 'The ethical argument depends on the economic worth of species.', 2, 'Ethical arguments are independent of economic worth, so II is false.');
stm(4, 'levels', 'Biodiversity occurs at three levels.', 'These levels are genetic, species and ecological diversity.', 0, 'Both are correct.');
mc(4, 'loss', 'How many of the following are alien species in India: Parthenium, Lantana, Eichhornia, Clarias gariepinus, banyan?', 'Four', ['Three', 'Five', 'Two'], 'Parthenium, Lantana, Eichhornia and Clarias are invasive alien species. Banyan is native.', null, 'statements');
mc(4, 'loss', 'How many of these are extinct: dodo, quagga, thylacine, Steller\'s sea cow, passenger pigeon, Indian elephant?', 'Five', ['Four', 'Six', 'Three'], 'The first five are extinct. The Indian elephant still exists.', 'The last item is the catch.', 'statements');
match(4, 'conservation', 'xm4', 'Place and sacred grove or hotspot', ['A. Khasi and Jaintia Hills', 'B. Aravalli Hills', 'C. Western Ghats and Sri Lanka', 'D. Indo-Burma'], ['p. Meghalaya sacred groves', 'q. Rajasthan sacred groves', 'r. A biodiversity hotspot including peninsular India', 's. A biodiversity hotspot including the northeast'], 'A-p, B-q, C-r, D-s', ['A-q, B-p, C-r, D-s', 'A-p, B-q, C-s, D-r', 'A-r, B-q, C-p, D-s'], 'The two sets of groves are in Meghalaya and Rajasthan. The two hotspots are Western Ghats-Sri Lanka and Indo-Burma.');
match(4, 'patterns', 'xm5', 'Group and a measure', ['A. Genetic diversity', 'B. Species diversity', 'C. Ecological diversity', 'D. Z value in small areas'], ['p. Varieties of mango', 'q. Number of amphibian species in two regions', 'r. Desert, rain forest and mangrove', 's. 0.1 to 0.2'], 'A-p, B-q, C-r, D-s', ['A-q, B-p, C-r, D-s', 'A-p, B-q, C-s, D-r', 'A-r, B-q, C-p, D-s'], 'Mango varieties show genetic diversity. Species counts show species diversity. Different ecosystems show ecological diversity. Z is 0.1-0.2 in small areas.');
match(4, 'loss', 'xm6', 'Cause of loss and an example', ['A. Habitat loss', 'B. Over-exploitation', 'C. Alien species invasion', 'D. Co-extinction'], ['p. Clearing of the Amazon forest for soyabeans', 'q. Passenger pigeon', 'r. Nile perch in Lake Victoria', 's. A parasite lost with its host'], 'A-p, B-q, C-r, D-s', ['A-q, B-p, C-r, D-s', 'A-p, B-q, C-s, D-r', 'A-r, B-q, C-p, D-s'], 'Each example fits the cause listed.');
pass('xp2', 'Passage: an island survey', 'A student studies the number of plant species on islands of different sizes in the same sea. She plots S against A on both arithmetic and logarithmic axes. On the arithmetic graph the points form a rectangular hyperbola, and on the log graph a straight line of slope 0.2.');
pq('xp2', 3, 'patterns', 'The log-log graph shows', 'a straight line with slope Z = 0.2', ['a curve', 'a vertical line', 'a circle'], 'The relationship log S = log C + Z log A is linear on log axes.');
pq('xp2', 4, 'patterns', 'If area A is multiplied by 100, S is multiplied by about', '2.5', ['20', '100', '1.2'], '100<sup>0.2</sup> = 10<sup>0.4</sup> = 2.51.');
pq('xp2', 5, 'patterns', 'What does a slope of about 1.15 suggest if found for continents?', 'The effect of additional habitats and ranges in larger areas', ['A mistake in measurement', 'No habitat change', 'Species decline with area'], 'Very large areas have steeper slopes (0.6 to 1.2), because new habitats keep adding species.');
pass('xp3', 'Passage: a lake story', 'A lake once had over 200 species of small cichlid fish that were found nowhere else. A large predatory fish was introduced to improve fisheries. After some years most of the cichlids disappeared. The lake also became choked with floating weed. Villagers say that a rare fish was also caught in huge numbers.');
pq('xp3', 3, 'loss', 'The cause of loss of the cichlids is', 'alien species invasion', ['over-exploitation of the cichlids', 'habitat fragmentation', 'co-extinction only'], 'The introduced predator, the Nile perch in Lake Victoria, ate the native fish.');
pq('xp3', 4, 'loss', 'The floating weed in lakes of India is likely to be', '<i>Eichhornia</i> (water hyacinth)', ['Parthenium', 'Lantana', 'Banyan'], 'The water hyacinth is an invasive aquatic weed.');
pq('xp3', 5, 'loss', 'The word "endemic" for the cichlids means they were', 'found only in that region', ['found everywhere', 'found only in the sea', 'introduced'], 'Endemic species are restricted to a particular region.');
nat(4, 'patterns', 'In the species-area relation S = C A<sup>Z</sup> with C = 5 and Z = 0.25, how many species are expected in an area of 625 km<sup>2</sup>?', 25, '625<sup>0.25</sup> = 5 (since 5<sup>4</sup> = 625). S = 5 × 5 = 25.', null, 'Take the fourth root.');
nat(4, 'patterns', 'With Z = 0.25, a habitat is reduced to 1/10000 of its original area. By what percentage does the number of species fall?', 90, '(1/10000)<sup>0.25</sup> = 1/10. So species fall to 10 per cent, a loss of 90 per cent.', '%', 'A tiny area still keeps some species because Z is small.');
nat(4, 'patterns', 'With Z = 0.25, the area is made 4 times larger. By what percentage does the number of species rise? Give one decimal.', 41.4, '4<sup>0.25</sup> = 2<sup>0.5</sup> = 1.414. The rise is 41.4 per cent.', '%', 'The rise in species is much smaller than the rise in area.', 0.1);

